# Target C-II — teeth versus double-unit loaded corners

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Status:** proof-only geometric consequence; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core near-disk Type-II branch; low-`t` constants additionally use the proof-only minimax atomic gap `0.0021` / §171  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The outer-tooth / long-side dichotomy can be strengthened to a split involving only
actual local source geometry.  Large metric long-side mass forces compensating
sub-two side deficit.  Small outer detour prevents that deficit from hiding on
nonunit sides, so it must reappear as exact unit loaded-hull sides.  Counting adjacent
pairs of those unit sides produces linearly many double-unit loaded corners.

The final exact inequality is

\[
\boxed{
N_2+C_{11}\ge 2q-P_F-3d_{out},
}
\tag{DC.1}
\]

where

- `N_2` is the number of genuine two-edge outer teeth;
- `C_11` is the number of loaded force-hull vertices whose two adjacent hull sides
  both have length exactly one;
- `q` is the number of loaded hull vertices;
- `P_F=per(F)`; and
- `d_out=b-P_F` is the metric outer detour.

Thus the low-`t` branch must contain either a macroscopic tooth population or a
macroscopic population of exact double-unit loaded corners.  The latter corners have
a further local rigidity property: except at at most six high-turn corners, a
third contact, if present, is a distinct loaded-to-unloaded unit edge with inward
component greater than `sqrt(3)/2`; if it is absent, the corner contributes one full
unit of loaded contact-degree slack.

## 1. Positive and negative side-length excess

List the `q` consecutive loaded force-hull side lengths as

\[
s_1,\ldots,s_q,\qquad s_i\ge1.
\]

Put

\[
L_{>2}:=\sum_i(s_i-2)_+,
\qquad
D_{<2}:=\sum_i(2-s_i)_+.
\tag{DC.2}
\]

Since

\[
\sum_i(s_i-2)=P_F-2q,
\]

we have the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
D_{<2}=L_{>2}+2q-P_F.
}
\tag{DC.3}
\]

Let

\[
U_1:=\#\{i:s_i=1\}.
\]

Then

\[
D_{<2}
=U_1+\sum_{1<s_i<2}(2-s_i).
\tag{DC.4}
\]

For every nonunit side with `1<s_i<2`, the distinguished outer route has integer
length `ell_i>=2`, so

\[
2-s_i\le\ell_i-s_i.
\]

The outer routes partition the occurrence-counted old outer walk and

\[
\sum_i(\ell_i-s_i)=d_{out}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\sum_{1<s_i<2}(2-s_i)\le d_{out}.
\tag{DC.5}
\]

Equations (DC.3)--(DC.5) give

\[
\boxed{
U_1\ge L_{>2}+2q-P_F-d_{out}.
}
\tag{DC.6}
\]

This is a finite statement.  No asymptotic rounding argument is needed.

## 2. Unit sides force double-unit corners

Mark each of the `q` hull sides as unit or nonunit.  A loaded hull vertex fails to
have two adjacent unit sides only if one of its two incident hull sides is nonunit.
There are `q-U_1` nonunit sides, and each can spoil at most two vertices.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
C_{11}\ge q-2(q-U_1)=2U_1-q.
}
\tag{DC.7}
\]

Combining with (DC.6),

\[
\boxed{
C_{11}\ge2L_{>2}+3q-2P_F-2d_{out}.
}
\tag{DC.8}
\]

The preceding exact tooth/long-side theorem gives

\[
N_2+2L_{>2}\ge x_B-2d_{out},
\tag{DC.9}
\]

where

\[
x_B=b-q=P_F+d_{out}-q.
\tag{DC.10}
\]

Add (DC.8) and (DC.9).  The long-side currency cancels:

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_2+C_{11}
&\ge x_B-2d_{out}+3q-2P_F-2d_{out}\\
&=2q-P_F-3d_{out}.
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (DC.1).

The significance is that both terms on the left count finite, actual source
configurations.  `L_{>2}` has disappeared.

## 3. Low-`t` normalized form

On a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

Type II gives

\[
{q\over D}=t+\kappa,
\qquad
{P_F\over D}\to\pi.
\]

Thus (DC.1) yields

\[
\liminf {N_2+C_{11}\over D}
\ge 2t+2\kappa-\pi-3\delta.
\tag{DC.11}
\]

The proof-only minimax atomic-gap theorem and audited §171 low-`t` squeeze give

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t),
\qquad
R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0,
\tag{DC.12}
\]

with

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

At fixed `t`, the minimum of `2 kappa-3 delta` under (DC.12) occurs at
`kappa=0`, `delta=R(t)/2`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+C_{11}\over D}
\ge G_{11}(t)
:=2t-\pi-{3\over2}R(t).
}
\tag{DC.13}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
G_{11}(t)=2\pi+{3\over2}C_0-{19\over4}t.
}
\tag{DC.14}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\max\{N_2,C_{11}\}
\ge {G_{11}(t)\over2}.
}
\tag{DC.15}
\]

At the current proof-only floor

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279
=1.82124143797944\ldots,
\]

one has

\[
G_{11}(t_{21})\approx0.4923768973,
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_2,C_{11}\}
\ge0.2461884486\,D-o(D).
}
\tag{DC.16}
\]

The decimal is only an orientation value; (DC.13)--(DC.15) are the theorem.

## 4. Geometry of a double-unit loaded corner

Let `A,B,C` be consecutive loaded force-hull vertices with

\[
|A-B|=|B-C|=1.
\]

Let `phi in(0,pi)` be the exterior turn at `B`, so the hull interior angle is
`pi-phi`.

The total exterior turn of the convex force hull is `2pi`.  Hence at most six loaded
hull vertices can have

\[
\phi\ge\pi/3.
\tag{DC.17}
\]

Fix a double-unit corner with `phi<pi/3`.  Loaded contact degree is at most three.
Since `BA` and `BC` are already contacts, `B` has contact degree either two or three.

### Degree two

If `deg_C(B)=2`, then `B` contributes exactly one to

\[
s_B:=\sum_{b\in B}(3-\deg_Cb).
\tag{DC.18}
\]

### Degree three

Suppose `deg_C(B)=3` and let `W` be the third contact neighbor.

Canonical full-core loaded-chord elimination says every loaded-loaded unit contact is a
loaded hull side.  Since the only hull-side neighbors of `B` are `A,C`, the third
neighbor `W` is unloaded.

All positive-core vertices lie in `F`, so the ray `BW` lies inside the hull interior
cone between `BA` and `BC`.  Unit separation and triangle-freeness give

\[
\angle ABW>\pi/3,
\qquad
\angle WBC>\pi/3;
\tag{DC.19}
\]

equality in either would make a unit equilateral triangle with `B`.

Let `n_B` be the unit vector along the inward angle bisector of the hull interior cone
at `B`.  The cone has width `pi-phi`; after deleting angular margins `pi/3` from both
boundary rays, the possible third-contact directions lie within angular distance

\[
{\pi-\phi\over2}-{\pi\over3}
={\pi\over6}-{\phi\over2}
\]

of `n_B`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
(W-B)\cdot n_B
>\cos\left({\pi\over6}-{\phi\over2}\right)
\ge {\sqrt3\over2}.
}
\tag{DC.20}
\]

Thus a degree-three double-unit corner supplies a strongly inward, loaded-to-unloaded
unit contact.

## 5. Distinct inward-contact count

Let `E_in` be the set obtained by taking the unique third contact from every
`C_11` corner with exterior turn below `pi/3` and contact degree three.

The edges are distinct.  Their loaded endpoints `B` are distinct, and loaded-chord
elimination ensures the other endpoints are unloaded.

At most six double-unit corners are excluded by (DC.17).  Every remaining degree-two
corner contributes one to `s_B`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
s_B+|E_{in}|\ge C_{11}-6.
}
\tag{DC.21}
\]

Combining with (DC.1) gives a fully physical three-currency burden:

\[
\boxed{
N_2+s_B+|E_{in}|
\ge2q-P_F-3d_{out}-6.
}
\tag{DC.22}
\]

Every edge counted by `E_in` has the strong inward component (DC.20).

In the low-`t` regime,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\max\{N_2,s_B,|E_{in}|\}
\ge {G_{11}(t)\over3}.
}
\tag{DC.23}
\]

At `t=t_21`, the right side is approximately

\[
0.1641256324.
\tag{DC.24}
\]

So the current floor cannot hide simultaneously from all three actual structures:
outer teeth, loaded degree deficiency, and strongly inward loaded-to-unloaded contacts.

## 6. Optional conversion of the tooth currency

The shallow-tooth penetration theorem gives, for every fixed

\[
0<\eta<2(1-\cos(\pi/12)),
\]

\[
E_{pen}(\eta)
\ge {1\over4}\left(N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}\right).
\tag{DC.25}
\]

Using (DC.22), this implies

\[
\boxed{
4E_{pen}(\eta)+s_B+|E_{in}|
\ge2q-P_F-3d_{out}-{d_{out}\over\eta}-6.
}
\tag{DC.26}
\]

At the current floor, `delta<=0.002837775+o(1)`.  Taking `eta=0.06` gives a positive
linear lower bound on the maximum of the three contact/curvature currencies on the
left.  No numerical value is needed for the theorem.

## 7. Scope audit

- The derivation of (DC.1) is finite and occurrence-safe.  It uses the physical hull
  side lengths and the distinguished outer routes, not a simple-boundary assumption.
- `C_11` counts exact unit hull sides on both sides of the corner; no near-unit side is
  treated as a contact.
- The third-contact direction lemma uses only convexity, unit separation,
  triangle-freeness, the loaded degree cap, and canonical loaded-chord elimination.
- At most six corners are discarded by the exact total-turn budget.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the source.  They play no role in the
  positive-core degree statement.
- The low-`t` constants inherit the proof-only minimax-atomic-gap lifecycle.
- No KKT multiplier floor is used.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` source has been
  produced.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural theorem.  C-II and Level I remain open.
